title: Proposition 47
---
order: 47
---
short_label: I.47
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: In right-angled triangles the square on the side opposite the right angle equals the sum of the squares on the sides containing the right angle.
----
proof:

Let {ABC} be a right-angled triangle having the angle {BAC} right.

I say that the square on {BC} equals the sum of the squares on {BA} and {AC}.

Describe the square {BDEC} on {BC}, and the squares {GB} and {HC} on {BA} and {AC}. Draw {AL} through {A} parallel to either {BD} or {CE}, and join {AD} and {FC}. [!just I.46; I.31, I.Post.1]

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="320" height="380" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img src="propI47.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Pythagoras. The theorem is a SUM, so the colours decompose it the way
    // I.45 did: AL splits the big square BDEC into the rectangles BL and CL;
    // BL equals the square GB (orange) and CL equals the square HC (green), so
    // the big square visibly IS orange + green and those match the two smaller
    // squares. BDEC itself is left UNFILLED — its own two rectangles fill it,
    // the same trick as I.41's parallelogram. The congruent triangles ABD and
    // FBC, the hinge of the whole proof, share yellow.
    //
    // Joyce's own figure keeps the three squares as unfilled outlines, so the
    // colour lives in SEPARATE fill-only regions (GBfill / HCfill / BL / CL /
    // ABD / FBC), all initiallyHidden. The resting figure is therefore exactly
    // his; the colour arrives during the walk.
    //
    // A is a circleSlider on the circle with diameter BC, so angle BAC stays
    // right however the figure is dragged (Thales) — Joyce's touch, kept.
    //
    // Five of the seven markers sit at B (the I.13 situation). They tile
    // without overlapping — DBC (-90..0), ABC (0..52), FBA (52..142) — so all
    // three pin at r=20; the two WHOLE angles DBA and FBC each span two of
    // those and overlap each other, so they go to outer rings at 30 and 38.
    // Red = right angle, yellow = the added angle, magenta = the equal pair.
    var GIVEN = ["A","B","C","AB","BC","AC","ABC","angBAC"];
    var SQ    = GIVEN.concat(["D","E","F","G","H","K","A2","B2","C2",
                              "AG","AH","BD","FB","GC","CE"]);
    var LIN   = SQ.concat(["L","J","AL","AD","CF"]);
    var STR   = LIN.concat(["angBAG"]);
    var ANGB  = STR.concat(["angDBC","angFBA","angABC","angDBA","angFBC"]);
    var TRI   = ANGB.concat(["ABD","FBC"]);
    var EQ1   = TRI.concat(["BL","GBfill"]);
